export const godOfPleasureChapters: BookChapter[] = [
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-introduction",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 1,
    "title": "Introduction — The Question Religion Was Afraid to Ask",
    "body": "Why should the word *pleasure* make a religious person uncomfortable? Why is pain so often treated as though it were more spiritual than joy? Why have people learned to suspect delight, to distrust enjoyment, and to imagine that holiness must mean the absence of happiness?\n\nThere are reasons for caution. Human beings can desire what harms them. A person can enjoy domination, deception, cruelty, intoxication, exploitation, or the humiliation of another. The fact that an experience feels pleasurable does not prove that it is good. But the opposite conclusion is equally false: the existence of corrupt pleasures does not make pleasure itself corrupt.\n\nA category must not be condemned because some instances within it are evil. Food can nourish or poison. Power can protect or oppress. Speech can tell truth or deceive. Sexuality can be expressed in covenantal love or in exploitation. The misuse of a good does not establish that the good itself is evil.\n\nThe governing question of this book is therefore not, “Can pleasure be abused?” Of course it can. The governing question is, “What is pleasure in the order of God's creation, and what does the Creator reveal about its place in human life?”\n\nRevelation 4:11 places the question before the throne. The living creatures and elders do not pronounce God worthy because He has satisfied a human theory. They declare His worthiness because He is the Creator and because all things exist by His will and for His pleasure. The verse directs attention away from human speculation and toward divine purpose.\n\nJohn 10:10 brings the question into the mission of Christ: “I am come that they might have life, and that they might have it more abundantly.” Abundant life is not a licence for selfish excess. It is life in the fullness of the purpose of God. Christ does not introduce a rival purpose to creation; He comes as the one through whom life is given, restored, and made abundant.\n\nThe thesis of this book is that pleasure, understood as delight in what is truly good, belongs to the purpose of God for creation and human existence. The highest pleasure cannot be separated from the highest good. Since God is good, the fullest and most enduring delight is found in rightly relating to Him and in participating in the good He wills.\n\nThis thesis challenges two opposite errors. The first is religious suspicion: the idea that pleasure is inherently worldly, that God is glorified by human misery, or that holiness requires a permanent hostility toward enjoyment. The second is ungoverned hedonism: the idea that whatever an individual desires or enjoys is therefore justified. Neither follows from Revelation 4:11.\n\nThe chapters that follow define pleasure, examine the scriptural language of divine delight and human enjoyment, distinguish good pleasure from corrupt gratification, and consider the moral, personal, social, and political consequences of recovering pleasure as a subject of serious thought.\n\n---"
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-02",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 2,
    "title": "The Throne and the Declaration of Purpose",
    "body": "Revelation 4 is a vision of the throne of God. Its setting matters. The declaration of verse 11 is not spoken in a marketplace, a court of human opinion, or a private conversation about personal preference. It belongs to a scene of heavenly worship in which the Creator's worthiness is acknowledged.\n\nThe elders say: “Thou art worthy, O Lord, to receive glory and honour and power.” They then give the reason: “for thou hast created all things, and for thy pleasure they are and were created” (KJV). Worthiness, glory, honour, power, creation, and pleasure are joined in one declaration. The verse does not present creation as purposeless material that God later tries to make meaningful. Creation exists because of Him and in relation to His will.\n\nThe wording of the King James Version deserves careful handling. The Greek phrase commonly represented in modern translations as “because of your will” uses the word *thelēma*, meaning will, desire, or that which is willed. The KJV renders the thought “for thy pleasure.” This lexical observation does not cancel the KJV's theological force; it helps the reader understand the relationship between divine will and divine pleasure. God's pleasure is not an arbitrary mood detached from His character. It is bound to what He wills as the good Creator.\n\nThe argument must therefore be made carefully. Revelation 4:11 explicitly declares that all things were created by God's will and that their existence is related to His pleasure. The further claim that pleasure is the purpose of all things is the theological conclusion this book develops from that declaration and from the wider scriptural witness. It is not necessary to pretend that one English word settles every philosophical question. The strength of the argument lies in following the declaration through Scripture.\n\nWhy is God worthy? Not because creation grants Him worth, as though He depended on creatures to become glorious. He is worthy because He is the Creator. The created order owes its existence to Him. Glory and honour are fitting responses to the One from whom all things derive their being.\n\nThis establishes an order: God is not judged by creation; creation is understood in relation to God. Human beings do not invent the final meaning of pleasure and then impose it upon the Creator. They must learn what pleasure means in the light of the Creator's character and purpose.\n\nThe throne therefore confronts every account of existence that treats life as accidental, meaningless, or self-authorizing. It also confronts religious accounts that speak as though God created only to demand service while remaining indifferent or hostile to the good of His creation. The declaration is richer: the Creator wills, creates, sustains, and takes pleasure.\n\nThe first conclusion is foundational: **existence is purposeful, and its purpose must be understood from the One who created it.**"
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-03",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 3,
    "title": "Who Is the God Whose Pleasure Matters?",
    "body": "A discussion of pleasure becomes confused when it begins with human appetite and then projects that appetite onto God. The God of Scripture is not a magnified human consumer. He does not seek pleasure because He is incomplete, bored, needy, or dependent upon creation to repair a deficiency in Himself.\n\nGod's pleasure must be understood in relation to who He is. Scripture describes Him as good (Psalm 100:5; Mark 10:18), righteous (Psalm 11:7), merciful (Exodus 34:6–7), truthful (Titus 1:2), loving (1 John 4:8), and wise (Romans 16:27). His will is not morally empty. He does not call evil good because He happens to desire it. His pleasure is consistent with His nature.\n\nThis matters because the word *pleasure* can refer to very different things. A person may take pleasure in beauty, friendship, discovery, justice, music, food, rest, generosity, or the flourishing of a child. Another may take pleasure in cruelty, manipulation, or the defeat of a rival. The emotional category is similar; the moral objects are not. We must ask not only whether someone experiences pleasure, but what is being enjoyed, by whom, at whose expense, and in what relation to truth and goodness.\n\nThe Scriptures speak of God taking pleasure in His people (Psalm 149:4), in those who fear Him and hope in His mercy (Psalm 147:11), and in the prosperity or well-being of His servant (Psalm 35:27). Zephaniah 3:17 portrays God rejoicing over His people with joy. Luke 12:32 says it is the Father's good pleasure to give the kingdom. These are not pictures of a God who is emotionally hostile to the good of His creatures.\n\nGod's pleasure is not selfishness. Selfishness seeks one's own gratification by disregarding the good of others. God's pleasure is the delight of perfect goodness; it does not compete with the good of His creation. When He wills what is good, His creatures are not rivals to His joy. Their flourishing can be an object of His delight.\n\nThe claim that God is good is therefore central, not incidental. If God were cruel, His pleasure would be terrifying. But Scripture does not present cruelty as His essence. It presents Him as the good Creator whose judgments are righteous and whose mercy is real. The question of divine pleasure must be approached through that revelation, not through caricatures of God as a cosmic tyrant.\n\nTo call God the God of Pleasure is not to reduce Him to pleasure. It is to recognize that His creative will, His delight in goodness, and His purpose for creation belong together. He is the source, measure, and judge of what is truly good."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-04",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 4,
    "title": "What Is Pleasure? A Full Account of the Word",
    "body": "Pleasure is a broad human experience and a philosophical category. It cannot be reduced to one sensation. It includes delight, enjoyment, satisfaction, gladness, gratification, contentment, joy, appreciation, fulfilment, and the positive experience of attaining or participating in something regarded as good.\n\nPleasure may be bodily, emotional, intellectual, relational, aesthetic, moral, spiritual, or contemplative. The pleasure of a meal is not identical to the pleasure of understanding a difficult truth. The pleasure of rest is not the same as the delight of reconciliation. The enjoyment of music differs from the satisfaction of completing a just task. Yet all belong to the broad field of positive experience.\n\nPleasure can be immediate or reflective. Immediate pleasure is felt in the moment: the taste of food, the warmth of sunlight, laughter, a melody, physical comfort. Reflective pleasure arises when a person recognizes the meaning or goodness of something: the satisfaction of keeping a promise, the joy of seeing a student understand, the delight of a family reconciled, or the peace of having acted with integrity.\n\nPleasure can be sensory, but it is not only sensory. It can be intellectual: the delight of discovery, comprehension, invention, and insight. It can be relational: affection, friendship, belonging, trust, intimacy, and shared celebration. It can be moral: the satisfaction of justice, mercy, courage, generosity, and truth. It can be spiritual: worship, gratitude, communion with God, hope, and the joy of His presence.\n\nPleasure can also be described by its duration and depth. Some pleasures are brief and intense; others are quiet and enduring. Some satisfy a momentary appetite; others shape a whole life. Some depend upon external circumstances; others arise from settled character, meaningful relationships, and confidence in what is true.\n\nA comprehensive account must also distinguish pleasure from neighbouring concepts:\n\n- **Happiness** often describes a broader state of well-being or positive life experience, while pleasure may describe a particular delight or satisfaction.\n- **Joy** can include deep gladness that persists even when circumstances are difficult; it is not identical to momentary comfort.\n- **Gratification** is the satisfaction of a desire or appetite. It may be good or bad depending on the desire and the means of satisfying it.\n- **Contentment** is a settled sufficiency or peace, not necessarily the intensity of enjoyment.\n- **Fulfilment** concerns the realization of a capacity, purpose, commitment, or meaningful end.\n- **Delight** is a strong positive response to something valued; it may involve affection, admiration, wonder, or enjoyment.\n- **Satisfaction** is the experience that a need, desire, task, or expectation has been adequately met.\n- **Eudaimonia**, in philosophical discussion, concerns flourishing or living well; it is broader than a passing pleasurable sensation.\n\nPleasure may accompany the possession of a good, the exercise of a capacity, the appreciation of beauty, the completion of a worthy action, or the presence of someone loved. It may also arise from false beliefs and corrupt desires. That is why pleasure needs discernment.\n\nThe central definition advanced here is: **pleasure is the positive experience of delight, enjoyment, satisfaction, or fulfilment arising from an object, action, relationship, condition, or reality that a person experiences as desirable.** Its moral quality depends upon the nature of the object, the truth of the desire, the means by which it is pursued, and its consequences for the person and others.\n\nThis definition is intentionally wider than bodily sensation. A society that recognizes only consumption as pleasure has impoverished the concept. A religion that recognizes only suffering as spiritually serious has also impoverished it. Human beings can delight in truth, justice, wisdom, beauty, love, work, rest, generosity, worship, and the flourishing of others."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-05",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 5,
    "title": "Pleasure, Goodness, and the Highest Good",
    "body": "The question “What is pleasurable?” is not the same as the question “What is good?” A person can enjoy what is destructive. The fact that an action produces pleasure does not, by itself, establish its moral worth. A complete ethical account must explain the relationship between pleasure and goodness.\n\nThis book argues that pleasure reaches its highest form when it is the delight of what is truly good. The highest good cannot be an isolated sensation, because sensations pass and can be produced by morally corrupt objects. Nor can the highest good be mere painlessness, because a life without pain could still be empty of love, truth, justice, purpose, and meaningful activity.\n\nIf God is the supreme good, then delight in God and in what accords with His goodness has a claim higher than temporary gratification. Psalm 16:11 speaks of fullness of joy in God's presence. Psalm 37:4 calls the reader to delight in the Lord. These texts do not describe God as the enemy of joy; they locate joy in the right object.\n\nPleasure is ethically significant because human beings are not indifferent machines. We pursue, avoid, love, fear, admire, and desire. The objects that attract us shape our habits and our character. An ethical life must therefore ask what should be loved, what should be desired, what should be enjoyed, and what kinds of pleasure should be rejected.\n\nThe book's claim that pleasure is the highest good must be understood in this ordered sense: not every pleasure is the highest good, but the fullest good is not hostile to delight. Goodness is not made good by a passing feeling; rather, rightly ordered pleasure is the fitting enjoyment of what is good. Pleasure divorced from goodness becomes an unreliable guide. Goodness divorced from all delight becomes a distorted account of the life God gives.\n\nThis distinction is essential to the book's use of the word *hedonism*. In philosophy, hedonism has several forms. Psychological hedonism claims that people are ultimately motivated by pleasure or the avoidance of pain. Ethical hedonism claims that pleasure is intrinsically good or that it has a central place in the moral evaluation of life. These positions are not identical, and neither should be confused with the popular caricature that hedonism means “do whatever feels good.”\n\nThe argument here is a theological account of pleasure: the Creator is the source of life and the proper measure of the good; therefore pleasure must be understood in relation to His will and character. If the term *divine hedonism* is used, it must mean that delight in the good has its source, order, and final measure in God—not that every appetite is self-justifying.\n\nA moral principle that celebrates one person's gratification while permitting another person's misery cannot claim to express the goodness of God. The pleasure worth defending is not the pleasure of exploitation. It is pleasure consistent with truth, justice, love, freedom from oppression, and the flourishing of persons."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-06",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 6,
    "title": "The Difference Between Pleasure and Indulgence",
    "body": "One of the strongest objections to a theology of pleasure is the obvious fact that people can use pleasure as an excuse for destructive behaviour. Scripture itself warns about this. Hebrews 11:25 speaks of the pleasures of sin. James 4:1–3 connects conflict with disordered desires. Second Timothy 3:4 describes people as lovers of pleasures more than lovers of God. These passages must be faced directly.\n\nThey do not establish that all pleasure is evil. They establish that desire can become disordered and that pleasure can be pursued against God. The phrase “pleasures of sin” already distinguishes pleasure in itself from pleasure attached to sin. Likewise, “lovers of pleasures more than lovers of God” identifies a question of priority and allegiance. The problem is not that a person experiences delight; the problem is that pleasure becomes an ultimate authority and displaces the good.\n\nIndulgence is not simply enjoyment. It is the ungoverned or excessive satisfaction of appetite, especially where the desire ignores truth, responsibility, proportion, or the well-being of others. A person may indulge anger, greed, vanity, lust, consumption, or the desire to dominate. The pleasurable feeling does not sanctify the object.\n\nThe distinction can be stated plainly:\n\n- Pleasure is a broad category of positive experience.\n- Appetite is a desire for some perceived good or satisfaction.\n- Indulgence is the pursuit or satisfaction of appetite without fitting restraint or moral order.\n- Sin is not defined by the intensity of enjoyment but by its relation to God's will and righteousness.\n- Discipline is not hatred of pleasure; it is the ordering of desire toward what is genuinely good.\n\nA person who refuses to exploit others is not rejecting pleasure. That person is rejecting a corrupt means of gratification. A person who chooses fidelity over betrayal is not choosing misery as a virtue. That person is protecting a deeper good. A person who exercises restraint may be preserving health, trust, freedom, and future joy.\n\nPain is not automatically virtuous, just as pleasure is not automatically righteous. Pain can warn, discipline, accompany sacrifice, or result from injustice. It can also be needless, cruel, and destructive. Pleasure can be wholesome or corrupt. Neither pain nor pleasure should be worshipped as an isolated absolute. Both must be understood in relation to truth and the good.\n\nThe book therefore rejects the false choice between holiness and enjoyment. Holiness is not permission for appetite to rule; neither is it a command to despise every form of delight. It is a life ordered toward God, in which desires are examined, disciplined, and directed toward what is good."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-07",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 7,
    "title": "The Pleasure of God in the Scriptural Record",
    "body": "The theme of divine pleasure does not rest on one verse alone. Scripture repeatedly describes what God delights in, what pleases Him, and what He wills for His people.\n\nPsalm 149:4 says that the Lord takes pleasure in His people. Psalm 147:11 says that the Lord takes pleasure in those who fear Him and hope in His mercy. Psalm 35:27 calls for the Lord to be magnified, “which hath pleasure in the prosperity of his servant.” These passages present divine pleasure as directed toward people and toward their good.\n\nZephaniah 3:17 describes the Lord in the midst of His people as mighty to save and rejoicing over them with joy. Isaiah 62:4 uses the language of delight in describing God's relation to His people. Luke 12:32 says, “it is your Father's good pleasure to give you the kingdom.” Philippians 2:13 says that God works in believers both to will and to do of His good pleasure. Ephesians 1:5 and 1:9 speak of the good pleasure of His will.\n\nThe cumulative picture matters. God is not portrayed as a being who takes pleasure only in commands being obeyed while remaining indifferent to the life of His people. His pleasure is connected to mercy, salvation, relationship, kingdom, obedience, and the fulfilment of His purpose.\n\nThe Scriptures also speak of what does not please God. He does not delight in wickedness (Psalm 5:4); He rejects injustice and hypocrisy; He calls for righteousness, mercy, and truth. The contrast is instructive. Divine pleasure is morally defined. It is not indiscriminate approval.\n\nA serious study must gather these passages and allow them to illuminate one another. The Bible Introduction supplied for this project emphasizes collecting the relevant biblical information across Scripture rather than treating the Bible as a systematic discourse on every topic in one location. This book follows that method: it begins with Revelation 4:11 and examines the wider record concerning God's will, delight, goodness, and the life He gives.\n\nThe result is not a God who is embarrassed by pleasure. It is a God whose delight is inseparable from His goodness and whose will gives creation its purpose."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-08",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 8,
    "title": "Human Pleasure: Gift, Responsibility, and Abundant Life",
    "body": "The biblical account of human life includes enjoyment. Ecclesiastes speaks of eating, drinking, and finding satisfaction in one's labour as gifts from God (Ecclesiastes 2:24; 3:12–13; 5:18–20). First Timothy 6:17 describes God as the One “who giveth us richly all things to enjoy.” These texts resist the claim that enjoyment is inherently suspect.\n\nThe gift, however, carries responsibility. Enjoyment is not an excuse to forget the Giver, to waste what has been entrusted, or to harm another person. The ability to enjoy food does not justify gluttony that destroys health or the exploitation of those who produce it. The pleasure of wealth does not justify oppression. The pleasure of intimacy does not justify coercion or betrayal. The enjoyment of authority does not justify domination.\n\nJohn 10:10 declares Christ's purpose in terms of life abundant. Abundance should not be reduced to possessions, constant excitement, or the fulfilment of every appetite. Life is more than accumulation. It includes reconciliation with God, truth, freedom from destructive bondage, meaningful relationships, purposeful work, peace, wisdom, and the capacity to give and receive good.\n\nChrist's words do not introduce a divine hostility toward human flourishing. They expose the thief's destructive work and announce life in abundance. This abundance is not measured merely by the number of pleasurable sensations in a day. It is the richness of life restored to its proper source and direction.\n\nThe claim that Jesus came to testify to or make manifest a purpose already present in the Creator's will should be developed with care. John 10:10 directly states His mission in terms of abundant life. Revelation 4:11 speaks of creation's relation to God's will and pleasure. The theological connection is that the life Christ gives is consistent with the Creator's purpose; it is not a rival to it. Where sin has damaged human life, Christ brings life rather than celebrating the damage.\n\nHuman pleasure is therefore a gift to receive with gratitude, a capacity to exercise with wisdom, and a responsibility to order toward the good. The mature person does not need to despise enjoyment to prove seriousness. Nor does the mature person surrender judgment to appetite. Maturity is the ability to recognize and pursue the pleasures that belong to a good life."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-09",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 9,
    "title": "The Ethics of Pleasure: The Good of the Other",
    "body": "If pleasure is to be defended as an ethical principle, the argument must pass beyond the private individual. A person may ask, “What gives me pleasure?” The ethical question asks, “What kind of pleasure can be pursued without denying the good, dignity, and rightful claims of another?”\n\nA pleasure founded on another person's degradation contradicts the goodness this book seeks to defend. Exploitation may gratify the exploiter, but it is not a worthy expression of the Creator's purpose. Cruelty may entertain a cruel person, but the experience does not become good because it is enjoyed. The pleasure of power must be distinguished from the goodness of responsible authority.\n\nThe principle is not that every person's preferences must be satisfied. Desires conflict, resources are finite, and some desires are unjust. The principle is that human beings should seek forms of enjoyment consistent with truth, justice, love, and the flourishing of others. The pleasure of one should not be purchased through the unjust destruction of another.\n\nThis gives social meaning to generosity. A person can take pleasure in another's success, recovery, learning, freedom, or happiness. Such delight expands rather than diminishes the good. It is the opposite of envy, which experiences another's good as a personal loss.\n\nFamilies are transformed when the pleasure of each member is not pursued at the expense of the others. Institutions become more humane when they recognize that people are not merely instruments of output. Economies are judged not only by production but by the quality of life they make possible and the harms they permit. Education should awaken the delight of understanding, discovery, competence, and contribution. Leadership should take pleasure in the flourishing of those entrusted to its care.\n\nThis is why pleasure has public consequences. A society that stigmatizes every enjoyment produces shame and hypocrisy. A society that worships gratification produces exploitation and exhaustion. A society ordered toward genuine good seeks conditions in which people can live, learn, create, work, rest, love, worship, and flourish without being crushed by the appetites or power of others."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-10",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 10,
    "title": "Kings, Power, and the Pleasure of a Just Society",
    "body": "Those who govern have a special responsibility because their decisions shape the conditions in which millions of people live. Power can create the conditions for flourishing, or it can manufacture misery. A ruler may take pleasure in wealth, praise, control, and the obedience of subjects. But these pleasures are not proof of worthy leadership.\n\nThe question Revelation 4:11 places before every ruler is ultimate: the Creator is worthy; the ruler is not the creator. Authority is therefore accountable. Human power is delegated, limited, and morally answerable. A king who treats people as instruments for personal gratification has confused office with ownership.\n\nA just ruler should take pleasure in the good of the people: security, honest courts, meaningful work, education, health, opportunity, peace, and the protection of the vulnerable. This is not sentimental politics. It is a practical recognition that authority exists within a moral order and that the well-being of people is not an incidental concern.\n\nThe prophets repeatedly condemn rulers who exploit the poor, manipulate justice, and use power for private gain. Scripture's criticism is not a criticism of pleasure as such; it is a criticism of pleasure detached from righteousness. The ruler's delight in luxury cannot excuse the misery of those whose labour or rights have been stolen.\n\nThe same principle applies to institutions, churches, businesses, and families. Leadership should not be measured by how much pleasure the leader extracts from the position. It should be measured by the good the position makes possible for those served.\n\nA society awakened to the Creator's purpose will ask different questions. Does our economy enable people to live meaningful lives? Does our education cultivate understanding and capability? Do our laws protect the weak from the strong? Do our institutions reward truth? Do our leaders take pleasure in the flourishing of the people or in their dependence and fear?\n\nKings should tremble—not because the book threatens them with theatrical language, but because it reminds them that power is not ultimate and that the Creator's goodness is the measure by which human authority is judged."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-11",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 11,
    "title": "The Stigma of Pleasure and the Awakening of Society",
    "body": "The stigma surrounding pleasure has often been produced by confusion. Some have treated the abuse of pleasure as proof that pleasure itself is evil. Others have used religious language to justify control, deprivation, or suspicion toward ordinary human enjoyment. At the opposite extreme, commercial culture has reduced pleasure to consumption and instant gratification. Both distort the human person.\n\nThe recovery of pleasure requires intellectual honesty. We must acknowledge the biblical warnings against corrupt desire while also acknowledging the biblical celebration of joy, delight, gratitude, feasting, beauty, rest, friendship, and the enjoyment of God's gifts. Neither group of texts should be silenced to protect a predetermined ideology.\n\nThis book challenges religious beliefs that portray God as an oppressor who begrudges human happiness. It also challenges cultural beliefs that define freedom as the right to satisfy every appetite without regard for consequence. The first produces needless guilt; the second produces bondage under desire. Neither is abundant life.\n\nA transformed person learns to desire what is good. Such a person does not merely ask whether something feels good now, but whether it is truly good, whether it is just, whether it builds life, whether it honours God, and whether it contributes to the flourishing of others. This is not the death of pleasure. It is the education of pleasure.\n\nA transformed society would not make suffering its badge of virtue or gratification its highest law. It would value the conditions in which people can experience meaningful joy: truthful relationships, just institutions, safe communities, purposeful work, access to knowledge, creative opportunity, rest, beauty, worship, and the freedom to develop their God-given capacities.\n\nThe awakening begins when pleasure is rescued from caricature. It is not inherently evil, and it is not self-authenticating. It is a profound dimension of human experience that must be understood from the Creator and ordered toward the good.\n\n---"
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-12",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 12,
    "title": "Creation Is Not Merely Being: The Goodness of Creaturely Life",
    "body": "Revelation 4:11 concerns all things, not merely the human capacity to think about God. The question is larger than whether an individual may enjoy a meal, music, friendship, or a day of rest. It concerns the character of the created order and the purpose for which it exists.\n\nGenesis 1 repeatedly presents God's assessment of creation as good. The repeated verdict is not a statement that every creature has the same function or that every created thing is pleasurable in the same way. It is the Creator's approval of an ordered creation that fulfils His intention. The final declaration that creation is “very good” (Genesis 1:31) gives the reader a foundation for rejecting the notion that material existence is inherently contemptible.\n\nPsalm 104 invites the reader to consider a world filled with provision, movement, food, water, shelter, work, and creaturely dependence. The psalm celebrates the Creator's wisdom and provision. Psalm 19 speaks of the heavens declaring God's glory. Psalm 65 describes the earth as watered and enriched. Psalm 145 celebrates God's open hand and the satisfaction of the desire of every living thing. These passages do not say that every event in nature is comfortable or that creation is free from disorder. They show that provision, beauty, abundance, and satisfaction belong within biblical praise of the Creator.\n\nThe distinction between existence and flourishing is important. To exist is to be; to flourish is to live in the proper development and exercise of one's capacities. A tree may exist while deprived of water; its existence is not the same as its flourishing. A person may remain alive while deprived of safety, affection, knowledge, meaningful work, or freedom from oppression. The fact of existence alone does not exhaust the good of life.\n\nThe book's thesis is not that every creature experiences pleasure in the same conscious way. Nor does it claim that every created event is pleasant to human beings. Rather, the Creator's purpose includes the goodness, order, provision, and flourishing of creation. Human pleasure is one dimension of that wider goodness, and it must be understood within it.\n\nThe supplied *Bible Introduction* describes the Bible as revealing God's dealings with humanity and says that “man's good” is its design and “the glory of God” its end. It also calls the Bible “a world of pleasure.” That phrase is a striking part of the source's description of Scripture itself. It does not independently prove every philosophical conclusion in this book; it demonstrates that the supplied study tradition does not treat pleasure as a forbidden subject. Its language joins the good of humanity and the glory of God rather than placing them in automatic opposition.\n\nThe same source warns that the Bible is not a systematic discourse on one subject. Its instruction must be gathered from different writers and passages. This is the method required here. Genesis, Psalms, Proverbs, the Prophets, the Gospels, and the Epistles each contribute to the subject. No single text should be forced to carry the entire weight of a doctrine when the wider record supplies the fuller account.\n\nThe conclusion is not that creation exists to satisfy human appetite. Human beings are not the measure of all things. The conclusion is that creation is not purposeless, and its goodness is not an embarrassment to divine holiness. The Creator's pleasure and the creature's proper good are not enemies."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-13",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 13,
    "title": "The Vocabulary of Pleasure: What the Words Can and Cannot Prove",
    "body": "A book about pleasure must take language seriously. English translations use several words—pleasure, delight, joy, gladness, satisfaction, desire, good pleasure, and enjoyment. These words overlap, but they are not interchangeable in every passage. The original languages also use more than one term. A doctrine cannot be established by treating every occurrence of an English word as though it translated the same Hebrew or Greek word.\n\nRevelation 4:11 is the first essential lexical control. The Greek text has the noun *thelēma* (θέλημα), commonly translated “will.” The King James Version renders the phrase “for thy pleasure”; many modern translations render it “because of your will.” The Cambridge Greek Testament commentary explains that the Authorized Version's “pleasure” is to be understood as “good pleasure,” not necessarily as the idea that God created things simply to entertain Himself. The lexical point is not a reason to discard the KJV. It is a reason to understand its older English use accurately: pleasure here is connected to God's will, purpose, and good pleasure.\n\nThat connection is central to the thesis. God's pleasure is not an impulse that competes with His wisdom. It is His good will in action. The Creator does not need to justify His will by appealing to a standard outside Himself; nevertheless, the Scriptures reveal His will as consistent with His character—good, righteous, faithful, and true.\n\nThe New Testament noun *hēdonē* (ἡδονή) is the source of the English word “hedonism.” It occurs five times in the Greek New Testament: Luke 8:14; Titus 3:3; James 4:1; James 4:3; and 2 Peter 2:13. In these contexts it is associated with pleasures or desires that choke fruitfulness, accompany former enslavement, wage war within people, motivate selfish requests, or characterize unrighteous conduct. These uses must not be concealed. They show that the New Testament can use *hēdonē* negatively when pleasure becomes disordered or controlling.\n\nSecond Timothy 3:4 uses a different word: *philēdonos* (φιλήδονος), “lover of pleasure,” formed from the idea of loving and pleasure. The verse condemns people who are “lovers of pleasures more than lovers of God” (KJV). The comparison is decisive. The text is not condemning every enjoyment; it condemns a love of pleasure that outranks love for God. It would be inaccurate to claim that this verse teaches that all lovers of pleasure are exemplary. It is equally inaccurate to use it to argue that all pleasure is evil. The moral issue is priority, object, and order.\n\nThe New Testament also uses *eudokia* (εὐδοκία) for good pleasure or gracious purpose in passages such as Ephesians 1:5, Ephesians 1:9, and Philippians 2:13. Hebrews 11:25 uses a different term, *apolausis*, in the phrase concerning the enjoyment of the pleasures of sin for a season. These distinctions matter because a doctrine of divine pleasure cannot be constructed by flattening the vocabulary into one undifferentiated word.\n\nThe Hebrew Scriptures likewise employ a range of terms for delight, desire, favour, and pleasure. Psalm 1:2 speaks of delight in the law of the Lord; Psalm 37:4 speaks of delighting in the Lord; Psalm 147:11 speaks of the Lord taking pleasure in those who fear Him and hope in His mercy. The precise Hebrew terms and grammatical forms differ. The passages should be read in context rather than treated as interchangeable proof texts.\n\nThe lexical conclusion is modest but important: Scripture has a rich vocabulary for divine good pleasure and human delight, while also warning against particular pleasures and disordered desire. The existence of negative uses does not make the whole category negative. The existence of positive uses does not make every desire good. Context, grammar, object, and argument determine the meaning in each passage.\n\nA further distinction is necessary. The philosophical term *hedonism* is a later label used for several theories about pleasure, motivation, and moral value. Scripture does not use the modern academic label as a technical doctrine. This book therefore does not claim that the historical philosophical school called hedonism began as a formal system in the Bible. It argues something more fundamental: pleasure as a created human experience, and delight in what is good, cannot be separated from the Creator who made all things and whose good pleasure is revealed in Scripture."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-14",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 14,
    "title": "The Cross, Self-Denial, and the Place of Pain",
    "body": "Any serious theology of pleasure must answer the question of suffering. If pleasure belongs to God's purpose, why does Scripture speak so frequently about pain, sacrifice, persecution, discipline, grief, and the cross? Does the Christian life not require self-denial? Did Christ not suffer?\n\nThe answer begins by refusing a false opposition. The existence of pain does not prove that pain is the highest good. The existence of pleasure does not prove that pleasure is the highest good in every form. Pain and pleasure are experiences; their moral meaning depends upon their context and relation to the good.\n\nPain can serve as warning. It can reveal injury, danger, injustice, or a need for change. It can accompany the cost of doing what is right. A parent may endure sleepless nights for a child; a physician may undertake difficult training to relieve suffering; a person may accept the discomfort of telling the truth rather than preserve a convenient lie. In such cases, the pain is not loved for its own sake. It is endured for the sake of a good that is judged more important.\n\nThe same distinction applies to self-denial. Biblical self-denial is not the denial that good things are good. It is the refusal to make the self, its appetites, or its immediate preferences the final authority. Jesus calls His disciples to take up the cross and follow Him (Matthew 16:24). The call is costly because loyalty to God may require surrendering what is desired in the moment. It does not teach that suffering is intrinsically superior to joy.\n\nHebrews 12:2 presents Jesus as enduring the cross “for the joy that was set before him.” The verse places suffering and joy in the same account. The joy did not make the cross painless; the pain did not erase the joy set before Him. The passage is not a licence to romanticize suffering. It shows that a worthy end can sustain endurance through suffering.\n\nThe cross also prevents a shallow account of abundant life. John 10:10 cannot mean that believers will never experience loss, illness, grief, persecution, or death. The New Testament itself records these experiences. Abundant life is not a promise of uninterrupted comfort. It is life in relation to Christ, a life whose source and hope are not destroyed by present affliction.\n\nThe doctrine of pleasure must therefore reject two errors. The first is pain-worship: treating deprivation, misery, or suffering as proof of holiness. The second is comfort-worship: treating any pain, restraint, or sacrifice as a violation of the good life. Both make an experience the final measure. Scripture instead directs the person toward faithfulness, righteousness, love, endurance, and the hope of life.\n\nIt is wrong to tell an oppressed person that suffering is automatically God's preferred condition for them. It is also wrong to promise that a faithful person will never suffer. The moral response to unjust suffering is not to praise the injustice; it is to oppose it, relieve it where possible, accompany those who endure it, and remain faithful to what is good.\n\nPleasure is not the denial of pain. It is the recovery of the good for which life is given. Sometimes the pursuit of that good requires endurance. Sometimes it requires rest, celebration, restoration, and delight. Wisdom knows the difference."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-15",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 15,
    "title": "Is Hedonism from God? Recovering the Claim Without Confusing the History",
    "body": "The title of this book deliberately places God and pleasure together. The argument also challenges the stigma attached to hedonism. But a serious work must distinguish the origin of pleasure from the history of a philosophical label.\n\nThe English word *hedonism* derives from Greek *hēdonē*, pleasure. In the history of philosophy, the label has been applied to different positions. Some accounts describe pleasure as the only intrinsic good; others describe human motivation in terms of pleasure and pain; still others distinguish immediate bodily gratification from more durable forms of enjoyment. Ancient schools associated with Aristippus and Epicurus did not hold identical positions, and modern utilitarian thinkers developed still other accounts of pleasure and welfare.\n\nThis book does not need to claim that God authored those historical schools, that every philosopher who used the term understood Scripture, or that every theory called hedonism is true. Such claims would confuse the Creator's relation to creation with the later history of human ideas.\n\nThe stronger theological claim is this: **pleasure itself is not the property of a secular ideology.** Human beings did not invent the capacity for delight. They did not create the world of taste, sound, beauty, friendship, bodily comfort, intellectual discovery, or relational joy. They did not create the good things that can rightly be enjoyed. These belong to the created order and must be understood in relation to the Creator.\n\nIf a philosophical school identifies pleasure as important, that observation does not make the school the source of pleasure. The source of the created capacity is prior to every human theory about it. A map is not the territory; a theory of pleasure is not the origin of pleasure.\n\nYet the book must not turn this argument into a shortcut. It is not enough to say “God created pleasure, therefore every hedonistic proposition is correct.” The conclusion does not follow. Theories must be examined. A position that makes an individual's immediate gratification the sole moral criterion cannot account adequately for justice, fidelity, truth, responsibility, or the claims of other persons. A position that treats pleasure as one dimension of a flourishing life may be closer to the argument developed here, but it too must be tested.\n\nThe book's proposal may be described as **theological hedonism** if the term is carefully defined: the Creator is the source and measure of genuine good; pleasure is a real dimension of the enjoyment of good; and human desire is to be ordered toward God and the good He reveals. This is not the claim that “whatever feels good is good.” It is the claim that goodness is not complete in a creaturely life that is permanently alienated from delight.\n\nThe phrase “lovers of pleasure” requires the same care. Second Timothy 3:4 does not commend those who love pleasure more than God. It exposes the disorder of a life in which pleasure has become a rival master. The book therefore calls people to become lovers of what is truly good and to delight in it—not to enthrone appetite. The most worthy person is not the person who gratifies every desire, but the person whose loves are rightly ordered and whose enjoyment does not depend on the diminishment of others.\n\nThat is the distinction on which the argument stands. Pleasure has its source in the Creator; the moral evaluation of a particular pleasure depends on its object, means, order, and fruit."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-16",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 16,
    "title": "The Modern Stigma: Religious Suspicion and Cultural Reduction",
    "body": "The modern world often speaks about pleasure in contradictory ways. Commercial culture sells pleasure as a product, promising that the next purchase, experience, image, or upgrade will finally satisfy. At the same time, religious communities may speak about pleasure as though enjoyment itself were dangerous. One side commodifies pleasure; the other may stigmatize it. Both can prevent a serious understanding of what pleasure is.\n\nThe word “woke” is used in public debate in different and often conflicting ways. It can refer to awareness of injustice, or it can be used as a broad polemical label for progressive cultural positions. This book does not treat the label as an argument. It identifies specific claims and evaluates them. Where a contemporary ideology reduces moral good to personal preference, treats every restraint as oppression, or dismisses the Creator's authority as inherently hostile to human freedom, it must be challenged. Where religious rhetoric portrays God as an enemy of human flourishing, it must also be challenged.\n\nA critique is only strong when it names the actual proposition it opposes. The book therefore refuses to use “woke” as a substitute for analysis. It asks: What is being claimed about the body? About desire? About freedom? About moral limits? About the good of others? About God? The answer must be examined rather than assumed.\n\nThe cultural reduction of pleasure to consumption is especially damaging. If pleasure is equated with buying, eating, sexual access, entertainment, or status, people are trained to become consumers rather than persons capable of deep delight. The pleasures of learning, craftsmanship, contemplation, friendship, worship, service, and creative contribution become less visible. A life can be full of stimulation and empty of fulfilment.\n\nReligious suspicion creates another distortion when it treats the body as though it were an obstacle to spirituality, or regards ordinary enjoyment as evidence of weak faith. Scripture does not support a blanket rejection of created goods. It calls for gratitude, moderation, discernment, and obedience. First Timothy 6:17 speaks of God giving richly all things to enjoy. Ecclesiastes repeatedly recognizes enjoyment of food, work, and life as gifts from God, while also confronting vanity and the limits of human achievement.\n\nThe answer to excess is not contempt for enjoyment. It is formation. The answer to shame is not indulgence. It is truth. The answer to the commodification of pleasure is not to deny that people need joy; it is to recover the forms of delight that cannot be purchased or monopolized.\n\nA society that takes pleasure seriously will ask whether its people have access to education, safety, meaningful work, clean environments, trustworthy relationships, rest, beauty, and the opportunity to develop their abilities. These are dimensions of a life in which human beings can flourish."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-17",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 17,
    "title": "The Public Measure: Economics, Work, Education, and the Flourishing of Nations",
    "body": "A theology of pleasure cannot remain a private sentiment. If human beings are created with capacities for relationship, learning, work, creativity, responsibility, and delight, then social arrangements matter. Poverty, corruption, violence, preventable disease, ignorance, and exploitation can deprive people of conditions in which life may flourish.\n\nThis does not mean that public policy can guarantee happiness or that governments should attempt to manufacture a single approved form of pleasure. Human beings differ in their vocations, cultures, temperaments, and lawful preferences. A just society protects the conditions in which people can pursue meaningful goods without being coerced or exploited.\n\nWork is a central example. Work can be a source of provision, skill, contribution, identity, and satisfaction. It can also become a site of exploitation when workers are treated as disposable instruments. A society that values human flourishing will ask not only how much output is produced, but whether work is safe, fairly organized, meaningful, and compatible with family and rest.\n\nEducation is another example. Education should not be reduced to credential collection or preparation for repetitive labour. It should cultivate perception, knowledge, reasoning, judgment, skill, creativity, and the delight of understanding. The 2015 Aletheia Libraries leaflet supplied for this work emphasizes the scholarly labour of Scripture, diligence in study, and the development of sound judgment through knowledge, perception, learning, and reasoning. That source is addressing Christian formation, not public education policy; nevertheless, its intellectual discipline is relevant to the book's argument that delight must be joined to understanding rather than left to impulse.\n\nLeadership must be judged by the same standard. A ruler's pleasure in wealth or power is not a measure of the people's good. The prophetic critique of unjust rulers and the biblical concern for the poor show that authority is accountable. A king should take pleasure in justice, peace, honest judgment, and the flourishing of the people. The ruler who enjoys the misery of subjects has not discovered a higher form of pleasure; he has exposed a corrupt desire.\n\nThe economic implications are substantial. Wealth can enable health, shelter, education, innovation, mobility, and generosity. But wealth can also become an instrument of domination. Economic success should therefore be evaluated in relation to the lives it makes possible and the harms it permits. The good of a nation is not exhausted by aggregate output, nor is it served by policies that celebrate consumption while neglecting dignity and opportunity.\n\nA society awakened to the Creator's purpose will not confuse austerity with righteousness or luxury with flourishing. It will seek the just conditions under which people can develop their capacities, contribute to the common good, and enjoy the fruits of honest labour."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-18",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 18,
    "title": "The Body, Desire, and the Gift of Enjoyment",
    "body": "A theology of pleasure must speak honestly about the body. Human beings do not exist as minds temporarily trapped in physical shells. Scripture presents embodied life as part of God's creative work. Genesis 1 describes the creation of male and female, the blessing of fruitfulness, and the provision of food. The body is not an accidental embarrassment to be escaped; it is part of creaturely existence.\n\nThis does not mean that every bodily desire is self-authenticating. Hunger does not justify theft. Sexual desire does not justify coercion, betrayal, or the use of another person. The body is good as created, but bodily appetites can be disordered. The moral question is not whether desire is physical; it is whether desire is truthful, rightly directed, and consistent with love and righteousness.\n\nFood offers a simple example. Scripture contains feasts, shared meals, provisions, and gratitude for what God supplies. The same Scriptures warn against gluttony, drunkenness, greed, and the neglect of the poor. The gift is not condemned because it can be abused. Rather, enjoyment is joined to gratitude, self-control, hospitality, and responsibility.\n\nSexuality requires the same seriousness. The goodness of embodied intimacy cannot be reduced to shame, nor can desire be used to erase covenant, fidelity, consent, or responsibility. The Bible celebrates marital love in the Song of Solomon and uses marriage as an image in several theological passages. It also condemns sexual exploitation, adultery, violence, and unfaithfulness. A mature theology of pleasure must neither demonize the body nor turn another human being into an instrument of gratification.\n\nRest is also a pleasure-bearing good. The Sabbath pattern in Scripture places limits on endless labour and recognizes rest as part of ordered life. Rest is not laziness; it is a refusal to treat human beings as machines whose only value is output. A society that denies rest to workers while celebrating the luxury of its rulers has confused productivity with the good of life.\n\nThe body can experience pain, fatigue, pleasure, desire, satisfaction, and delight. These experiences are not moral verdicts in themselves. They are part of embodied life and must be interpreted through wisdom. Pleasure becomes worthy when the person receives good things with gratitude, exercises restraint, honours others, and refuses to make appetite the ruler."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-19",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 19,
    "title": "The Pleasure of Knowing, Creating, and Working",
    "body": "Pleasure is not limited to consumption. One of the most overlooked forms of enjoyment is the delight of understanding. The moment when confusion gives way to insight, when a pattern becomes visible, when a difficult skill is mastered, or when a question receives a truthful answer can produce profound satisfaction.\n\nThe supplied 2015 Aletheia Libraries leaflet emphasizes the scholarly impetus of Scripture. It describes diligence in study and connects knowledge with perception, learning, reasoning, and the exercise of the Word. Its concern is Christian maturity, but its emphasis reveals a wider point: intellectual formation is labour, and labour can produce the pleasure of understanding. A person who has learned to think carefully can take delight in truth rather than merely in winning an argument.\n\nProverbs repeatedly praises wisdom and understanding. The pursuit of wisdom is not presented as a joyless burden. Proverbs 3:13 calls the person who finds wisdom and gets understanding blessed. Proverbs 8 personifies wisdom and speaks of delight in the inhabited world. These passages invite a view of intelligence and learning as goods that can be enjoyed.\n\nCreation and craftsmanship also carry pleasure. The artist delights in bringing form to an idea; the builder in completing a sound structure; the teacher in seeing a learner grasp a concept; the farmer in a harvest; the engineer in a system that works; the musician in the disciplined expression of sound. The satisfaction is not merely the reward or payment. It is also the fitting exercise of a human capacity.\n\nWork can be corrupted by exploitation, vanity, and endless accumulation. Ecclesiastes exposes the emptiness of labour when it becomes an attempt to secure ultimate meaning through possessions or achievement alone. Yet the same book recognizes enjoyment of one's work as a gift. The answer to meaningless toil is not the abolition of work; it is the recovery of work in its proper place.\n\nThis matters for education, enterprise, and national development. Institutions should not treat people as replaceable units of output. They should cultivate skill, creativity, judgment, and contribution. Work becomes more humane when it is safe, fairly rewarded, purposeful, and compatible with rest and relationships.\n\nThe pleasure of knowledge is also a safeguard against manipulation. People who delight in truth are less willing to repeat falsehood merely because it flatters them. They ask for evidence, distinguish what is known from what is assumed, and revise conclusions when the evidence requires it. Intellectual pleasure is therefore not ornamental; it strengthens moral agency.\n\nA society that wants flourishing must create room for discovery, craft, invention, teaching, and the honest satisfaction of doing good work. The Creator's gifts are not limited to what people consume. They include what people are able to learn, make, repair, cultivate, and contribute."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-20",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 20,
    "title": "The Pleasure of Love, Friendship, and Shared Life",
    "body": "Pleasure reaches beyond the individual because human beings are relational. Friendship, affection, family life, hospitality, mutual trust, reconciliation, and shared celebration are among the deepest sources of human delight. These goods cannot be reduced to private sensation. They arise between persons.\n\nScripture repeatedly places human beings within relationships of responsibility. The command to love one's neighbour prevents pleasure from becoming a private kingdom in which another person's welfare is irrelevant. The joy of a household, the peace of a community, and the trust of a friendship depend upon truthfulness, fidelity, patience, forgiveness, and mutual regard.\n\nThe pleasure of friendship is not merely having someone available for one's convenience. It includes being known, receiving honest counsel, sharing burdens, celebrating another's good, and remaining present when circumstances are difficult. A friend who enjoys another person's success is not diminished by it. Such pleasure expands the good rather than competing for it.\n\nHospitality gives pleasure a public form. A meal becomes more than consumption when it is shared. A home becomes more than property when it offers welcome. A community becomes more than a collection of individuals when people protect the vulnerable and create conditions in which others can belong. These acts can be joyful precisely because the good is shared.\n\nThe biblical picture of the kingdom includes a table, a feast, reconciliation, and the gathering of people. These images should not be flattened into a literal prediction about every detail of the future, but neither should their language of celebration be ignored. The kingdom is not portrayed merely as an endless lecture or a sterile existence without delight. It is associated with life, fellowship, joy, and the presence of God.\n\nThere is a moral test here. If a person's pleasure requires the isolation, humiliation, or subordination of others, it is not the kind of pleasure this book defends. If a person's joy increases when another person flourishes, it reflects a different order of desire. Love does not erase personal delight; it teaches people to delight in goods that can be shared.\n\nThis is also why social justice belongs in a book about pleasure. Injustice damages relationships and deprives people of the conditions for a meaningful life. The pursuit of pleasure rightly understood cannot be indifferent to exploitation, violence, discrimination, corruption, or preventable deprivation. The good of the other is not a distraction from pleasure; it is part of the moral order in which genuine pleasure can be pursued.\n\nThis is the precise sense in which the book calls the love of pleasure ethically worthy. A person who loves the delight of truth, the joy of another's flourishing, the satisfaction of justice, the beauty of creation, the fulfilment of meaningful work, and the shared happiness of a community is not morally inferior for loving pleasure. Such a person seeks pleasure in goods that can be shared and that do not require another person's degradation. To love pleasure for oneself and for others, when pleasure is understood as delight in what is truly good, is to affirm life rather than to glorify misery.\n\nThis is not the same as the person described in 2 Timothy 3:4, who loves pleasures more than God. The biblical warning concerns the ordering of love: pleasure has become a rival master. The ethical person described here loves God and therefore learns to love what accords with His goodness, including the good and rightful delight of other people. The distinction is not between people who enjoy and people who do not; it is between rightly ordered love and disordered allegiance.\n\nPain may be unavoidable, instructive, or endured for the sake of a greater good, but pain is not the moral end. The aim of justice is not to make people suffer; it is to restrain evil, repair harm, protect the vulnerable, and restore conditions in which life can flourish. The aim of discipline is not misery; it is formation. The aim of sacrifice is not pain for its own sake; it is fidelity to a good that matters more than immediate comfort. A society that treats needless suffering as a virtue has confused the means with the end."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-21",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 21,
    "title": "The Final Horizon: Joy, Hope, and the Life to Come",
    "body": "A theology of pleasure must consider more than the present moment. Human life is marked by change, loss, ageing, grief, and death. If pleasure is reduced to immediate sensation, it is fragile because circumstances can remove it. Scripture's hope reaches beyond the instability of present conditions.\n\nThe Bible speaks of joy in God's presence, the hope of resurrection, the restoration of creation, and the end of death and sorrow. Psalm 16:11 speaks of fullness of joy in God's presence. The New Testament places Christian hope in the resurrection of Christ and the resurrection of the dead. Revelation 21:1–4 portrays a renewed order in which death, sorrow, crying, and pain are no more.\n\nThese promises do not make present suffering unreal. They prevent suffering from becoming the final word. Christian hope does not require a person to pretend that grief is pleasure. It declares that grief and death do not have ultimate authority over God's purpose.\n\nThe future hope also corrects shallow definitions of abundance. Abundant life is not measured by continuous excitement, endless consumption, or the absence of every difficulty. It is life grounded in God, reconciled through Christ, and directed toward a future in which the good purpose of God is not finally defeated by evil and death.\n\nThe book's governing claim therefore has an eschatological horizon. The Creator's purpose is not exhausted by the temporary pleasures available in a fallen world. The promise of resurrection means that the good of embodied life is not discarded; it is redeemed. The hope of a renewed creation means that God's purpose concerns the whole created order, not merely the escape of disembodied souls from it.\n\nThe final horizon is not selfish gratification. It is communion with God, the restoration of what is broken, the defeat of evil, and the fullness of life. If pleasure means delight in what is truly good, then the promised end of God's work is not the triumph of misery. It is the fulfilment of His good purpose."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-22",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 22,
    "title": "The Objections That Must Be Answered",
    "body": "A book that argues for the moral importance of pleasure must answer the strongest objections rather than dismiss them.\n\n**Objection 1: If pleasure is central, does that not justify selfishness?** No. The argument distinguishes pleasure from selfish gratification. A pleasure that depends on deception, coercion, exploitation, or the destruction of another person's good is not justified merely because it is enjoyed. The moral object and the means matter. The good of another person is not an optional decoration added after one's pleasure has been secured.\n\n**Objection 2: Does Scripture not condemn lovers of pleasure?** Second Timothy 3:4 condemns those who love pleasures more than God. The comparison is explicit. The verse describes disordered allegiance, not a universal prohibition of enjoyment. Luke 8:14, Titus 3:3, James 4:1–3, and 2 Peter 2:13 likewise describe pleasures or desires that become spiritually unfruitful or destructive. These texts must be read alongside passages that describe God's delight in His people and the enjoyment of His gifts. The whole scriptural record is necessary.\n\n**Objection 3: Does suffering disprove a world created for God's pleasure?** Suffering is real and must never be minimized. Revelation 4:11 does not say every creature experiences uninterrupted comfort, nor does it explain every instance of suffering in one sentence. The wider biblical account includes human rebellion, injustice, mortality, lament, judgment, redemption, and promised restoration. A doctrine of divine pleasure must account for these realities, not pretend they do not exist. The existence of suffering raises profound questions about evil and providence; it does not by itself prove that pleasure is evil or that the Creator's purpose is meaningless.\n\n**Objection 4: Is the book merely redefining pleasure so that only morally good things count?** The book distinguishes the experience of pleasure from the moral quality of its object. This is necessary precision. People can enjoy harmful things. If “pleasure” means any felt gratification, some pleasures are destructive. If it means delight in what is genuinely good, moral evaluation is part of the definition. The book states both senses rather than confusing them.\n\n**Objection 5: Does Revelation 4:11 really say that all things were created for pleasure?** The KJV reads, “for thy pleasure they are and were created.” The Greek phrase includes *thelēma*, “will,” and is commonly translated “because of your will.” A Cambridge Greek Testament commentary notes that the KJV's “pleasure” is to be understood as “good pleasure.” The verse relates creation to God's will and good pleasure. The book's broader thesis—that creation's purpose includes the good and delight of the Creator—is developed from this verse together with the wider biblical witness. It does not pretend that the Greek noun *hēdonē* appears in Revelation 4:11; it does not.\n\n**Objection 6: Is pleasure really the highest ethical principle?** Not if “pleasure” means the unexamined satisfaction of any desire. That would make the pleasure of a tyrant morally equivalent to the joy of a person who protects the vulnerable. The argument is that the highest good is not hostile to delight, and that the fullest pleasure is found in what is truly good. Pleasure is ethically significant because it concerns what people love and pursue; it must be ordered by truth, righteousness, and love.\n\n**Objection 7: Does this argument make God dependent on creation for His pleasure?** No. Revelation 4:11 declares God's worthiness as Creator. It does not say that God was incomplete before creation or that creatures supply a deficiency in Him. Divine pleasure is not human need magnified. The book speaks of God's good will and delight, not dependence.\n\nThe objections sharpen the thesis. They do not destroy it. Pleasure must be distinguished from indulgence, divine will from arbitrary appetite, abundant life from uninterrupted comfort, and the history of philosophical hedonism from the theological claim that the Creator is the source of created delight."
  },
  {
    "id": "ch-god-of-pleasure-conclusion",
    "bookId": "book-god-of-pleasure",
    "number": 23,
    "title": "Conclusion — For Thy Pleasure",
    "body": "The declaration of Revelation 4:11 remains: all things were created by God's will and in relation to His pleasure. The Creator is worthy. Creation is purposeful. Pleasure cannot be dismissed as an embarrassing subject, because the scriptural witness places divine pleasure within the account of who God is and why all things exist.\n\nThe argument has not been that every desire is righteous, every gratification is beneficial, or every experience called pleasure should be pursued. Scripture itself rejects the pleasures of sin and warns against loving pleasure more than God. Those warnings do not abolish pleasure; they establish the need for truth, order, and moral discernment.\n\nThe central claim is stronger and more careful: pleasure rightly understood belongs to the good purpose of God. The highest delight is not found in rebellion against the Creator but in the enjoyment of what is truly good. The abundant life Christ announces is consistent with the Creator's purpose and calls human beings out of destructive bondage into life.\n\nFor individuals, this means refusing both shame-driven denial and appetite-driven slavery. For families, it means making room for joy, affection, safety, and mutual flourishing. For institutions, it means measuring success by the good they produce rather than the gratification of those who control them. For rulers, it means recognizing that authority is accountable to the Creator and should take pleasure in justice and the flourishing of the people.\n\nThe question is no longer whether pleasure belongs in serious theology, ethics, or public life. The question is whether we will understand it truthfully.\n\nThe God of Pleasure is not the patron of selfish excess. He is the good Creator whose will gives existence purpose, whose delight is consistent with His character, and whose life-giving purpose challenges every system that glorifies misery, exploitation, or empty gratification.\n\nThe invitation is to recover pleasure without surrendering truth, to pursue delight without abandoning righteousness, and to understand abundant life in the light of the One who created all things.\n\n**“Thou art worthy, O Lord, to receive glory and honour and power: for thou hast created all things, and for thy pleasure they are and were created.” — Revelation 4:11 (KJV)**"
  }
];
